# GitHub Linguist hints - keep the Languages pie chart on the repo page
# representative of human-authored code, not vendored/generated artifacts.
#
# Without these flags Linguist counts every tracked .html / .css / .js byte
# regardless of provenance, which buries the actual ~4 MB of Python under
# (a) the python-embed/ vendored Python 3.14 distribution
# (b) the _archive/ quarantine of removed code
# Reference: https://github.com/github-linguist/linguist/blob/master/docs/overrides.md
# The rule that used to head this list covered a tree of verbatim scraped
# third-party HTML under data/meta_build/refresh_*/_phase*_html/. That tree was
# removed from the working copy AND from history on 2026-09-07 ahead of the
# public flip, because it was never ours to redistribute. The glob is kept
# because the extractors can still produce local, untracked HTML there.
data/meta_build/**/*.html         linguist-generated=true
data/meta_build/**/_*_html/**     linguist-vendored=true
# (b) Vendored Python distro - not our code
python-embed/**                   linguist-vendored=true
# (c) Archived inert code (e.g. pre-T2 #8 tkinter overlays under
#     _archive/2026-05-01-audit/) - preserved for reversibility, not active
_archive/**                       linguist-vendored=true
# (d) Local data caches that occasionally get tracked
data/icons/**                     linguist-vendored=true
data/meta/ddragon_*.json          linguist-generated=true
# EOL policy - prevent CR-CR-LF / CRLF corruption of Python sources.
# core.autocrlf=true on the Windows fleet, combined with tools that
# re-add an already-CRLF blob, produced doubled-CR line endings in 21
# tracked .py files (2026-05-19 rc_facts.py incident). Forcing eol=lf
# keeps .py LF in the repo AND the working tree, overriding autocrlf
# so the corruption cannot recur fleet-wide.
*.py text eol=lf
# RM-284: same policy for Markdown, for a different reason - SIZE, not corruption.
# ROADMAP.md and CLAUDE.md are CI size-budgeted (tests/test_doc_size_budget.py)
# and tools/drift_guard.py warns at 90 pct, and BOTH measure stat().st_size on
# disk. Under core.autocrlf=true the blob is LF and the Windows working tree is
# CRLF, so the same content measures one byte per line larger here than it does
# in the blob or on Linux CI - 197 bytes for ROADMAP.md. That gap flipped the
# verdict in both directions: a lane worktree measured 73718 and passed while
# main measured 73915 and breached, and earlier a tool rewrote the file LF-only,
# measured 81917 and shipped green against a real checkout size of 82134.
# eol=lf makes on-disk equal the blob everywhere. Guarded by
# tests/test_md_line_endings.py.
*.md text eol=lf

# RM-284 follow-on (2026-09-03): the same pin for every other tracked TEXT type,
# so on-disk equals blob for all of them and no future size/digest check can
# disagree by checkout. Verified line-ending-INDEPENDENT before pinning, rather
# than assumed: the web digest at tests/test_web_ascii_sweep.py normalises with
# .replace(CRLF, LF) before hashing; tools/ds_feed_index.py digests a canonical
# JSON OBJECT parsed via read_text; dashboard/_static.compute_asset_hash hashes
# MTIMES, not bytes; and the SHARED_SHA256 cross-repo pins are .py, already
# covered above. Guarded by tests/test_text_line_endings.py, which reads this
# list off disk rather than hardcoding it.
*.json  text eol=lf
*.js    text eol=lf
*.mjs   text eol=lf
*.ts    text eol=lf
*.css   text eol=lf
*.html  text eol=lf
*.svg   text eol=lf
*.xml   text eol=lf
*.txt   text eol=lf
*.csv   text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf
*.toml  text eol=lf
*.ini   text eol=lf
*.ps1   text eol=lf
*.spec  text eol=lf

# DELIBERATELY NOT PINNED, each for its own reason - do not "finish the job".
# .bat / .cmd  Windows shell scripts. The 15 tracked .bat are ALREADY LF and
#              work; the 12 .cmd are CRLF. Pinning either way churns live
#              launch/restart scripts to buy nothing - no size or digest check
#              reads them.
# .log         rotated logs are immutable history; CLAUDE.md exempts them from
#              repo-wide sweeps for exactly this reason.
# .jsonl       append-only ledgers, same exemption. Renormalising a file that is
#              only ever appended to would rewrite records nobody re-read.
# LFS payloads keep the -text rule below; it is LAST so it wins over *.json.
data/daemon_slayer/laning_scenarios/**/*.json filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
